Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used  

• pizza_customer_orders  
  – order_id : primary key for one pizza order  
  – customer_id : customer who placed the order  
  – pizza_id : links to recipe / name tables (1 = “Meatlovers”, 2 = all others)  
  – exclusions : comma‑separated topping_id values to be removed  
  – extras : comma‑separated topping_id values to be added  
  – order_time : timestamp of the order (needed for grouping)

• pizza_names  
  – pizza_id : joins to pizza_customer_orders  
  – pizza_name : literal name used in the output string

• pizza_recipes  
  – pizza_id : joins to pizza_names / pizza_customer_orders  
  – toppings : comma‑separated topping_id list of default ingredients

• pizza_toppings  
  – topping_id : key that appears in recipes / extras / exclusions  
  – topping_name : readable ingredient name that must appear (alphabetically) in final list

• pizza_get_exclusions / pizza_get_extras  
  – row_id : “row ID” requested in the task (the same order can appear more than once)  
  – order_id : joins to pizza_customer_orders  
  – exclusions / extras : topping_id (already exploded into one row per topping)  
  – total_exclusions / extras_count : can be used to count duplicates

Typical conditions that will be needed  
• WHERE pc.order_id = pe.order_id (join orders to extras)  
• WHERE pc.order_id = px.order_id (join orders to exclusions)  
• WHERE pn.pizza_id = 1              (force Meatlovers id = 1)  
• WHERE topp.topping_id IN (…)       (translate ids to names)  

