Below are ten exploratory SQL queries (all limited to ≤20 rows) that progress from simple inspection to a mini‑prototype of the final join.  Each query is preceded by a short comment.

```sql
-- 1.  Show every distinct pizza name available
SELECT DISTINCT "pizza_name"
FROM "pizza_names"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2.  List all distinct topping names that can appear on any pizza
SELECT DISTINCT "topping_name"
FROM "pizza_toppings"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3.  Inspect each pizza recipe and its default topping string
SELECT "pizza_id",
       "toppings"
FROM   "pizza_recipes"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4.  Look at the first few raw customer orders
SELECT "order_id",
       "customer_id",
       "pizza_id",
       "exclusions",
       "extras",
       "order_time"
FROM   "pizza_customer_orders"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5.  Show the exploded list of extra toppings that customers requested
SELECT DISTINCT "row_id",
                "order_id",
                "extras",
                "extras_count"
FROM   "pizza_get_extras"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6.  Show the exploded list of excluded toppings that customers removed
SELECT DISTINCT "row_id",
                "order_id",
                "exclusions",
                "total_exclusions"
FROM   "pizza_get_exclusions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7.  Translate one recipe’s topping_id list into readable ingredient names (pizza_id = 1)
SELECT pt."topping_name"
FROM   "pizza_recipes"  pr
JOIN   "pizza_toppings" pt
       ON instr(',' || pr."toppings" || ',', ',' || pt."topping_id" || ',') > 0
WHERE  pr."pizza_id" = 1
ORDER  BY pt."topping_name"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8.  For a single order (order_id = 1) list the EXTRA topping names
SELECT pt."topping_name"
FROM   "pizza_get_extras" ge
JOIN   "pizza_toppings"   pt
       ON ge."extras" = pt."topping_id"
WHERE  ge."order_id" = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9.  For the same order (order_id = 1) list the EXCLUDED topping names
SELECT pt."topping_name"
FROM   "pizza_get_exclusions" gx
JOIN   "pizza_toppings"       pt
       ON gx."exclusions" = pt."topping_id"
WHERE  gx."order_id" = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10.  Prototype: assemble final ingredient list for order_id = 1 (no 2x logic yet)
SELECT ge."row_id",
       pc."order_id",
       pc."customer_id",
       pn."pizza_name" || ': ' ||
       group_concat(DISTINCT pt."topping_name", ', ')     AS "final_ingredients"
FROM   "pizza_customer_orders" pc
JOIN   "pizza_names"          pn  ON pn."pizza_id"  = pc."pizza_id"
JOIN   "pizza_recipes"        pr  ON pr."pizza_id"  = pc."pizza_id"
JOIN   "pizza_get_extras"     ge  ON ge."order_id"  = pc."order_id"
LEFT JOIN "pizza_get_exclusions" gx ON gx."order_id" = pc."order_id"
JOIN   "pizza_toppings" pt
     ON (   instr(',' || pr."toppings" || ',', ',' || pt."topping_id" || ',') > 0   -- in recipe
        OR pt."topping_id" = ge."extras")                                           -- or is an extra
WHERE  pt."topping_id" != gx."exclusions"                                            -- but not excluded
  AND  pc."order_id" = 1
GROUP  BY ge."row_id", pc."order_id", pc."customer_id", pn."pizza_name"
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries progressively explore all information needed (pizza names, default recipes, extras, exclusions, and topping translations) and culminate in a miniature join that demonstrates how to build the final ingredient string for one order.
